@article{ WOS:001512318100002,
Author = {Calvani, Silvia and Paoloni, Riccardo and Foderi, Cristiano and
   Frassinelli, Niccolo and Kirschner, Judith A. and Menini, Alessio and
   Galeotti, Glenda and Neri, Francesco and Marchi, Enrico},
Title = {Wildfire risk perception survey: insights from local communities in
   Tuscany, Italy},
Journal = {FIRE ECOLOGY},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {21},
Number = {1},
Month = {JUN 20},
Abstract = {BackgroundWildfire is a complex chemical, physical, and sociological
   phenomenon deeply rooted in the historical relationship between humans
   and fire. Today the wildfire risk is one of the human challenges.
   Effective management requires collaboration among multiple stakeholders
   across different levels. The risk perception and vulnerability at the
   local community level explain why and how individuals consider certain
   policies or mitigation behaviors. Thus, wildfire risk fits within the
   framework of socio-ecological systems.This study focused on four
   fire-prone areas in Tuscany, Italy, aiming to explore local wildfire
   risk perception. Risk perception is a social parameter, derived from
   media habits, memory, history, concerns, and beliefs. Two different
   surveys were used to consult two groups: experts (e.g., wildfire
   technicians, policymakers, business activities, and rural associations)
   and non-experts (e.g., random residents, students, and tourists), then
   compared to investigate possible gaps. Several questions were asked
   regarding demographics, relationship with the territory, current
   management system, relationship with fire and media, risk perception,
   and others.Results were compared according to the critical area or the
   type of respondents, and several analyses were conducted to identify
   weaknesses, strengths, and areas for improvement to raise awareness and
   lower the risk.BackgroundWildfire is a complex chemical, physical, and
   sociological phenomenon deeply rooted in the historical relationship
   between humans and fire. Today the wildfire risk is one of the human
   challenges. Effective management requires collaboration among multiple
   stakeholders across different levels. The risk perception and
   vulnerability at the local community level explain why and how
   individuals consider certain policies or mitigation behaviors. Thus,
   wildfire risk fits within the framework of socio-ecological systems.This
   study focused on four fire-prone areas in Tuscany, Italy, aiming to
   explore local wildfire risk perception. Risk perception is a social
   parameter, derived from media habits, memory, history, concerns, and
   beliefs. Two different surveys were used to consult two groups: experts
   (e.g., wildfire technicians, policymakers, business activities, and
   rural associations) and non-experts (e.g., random residents, students,
   and tourists), then compared to investigate possible gaps. Several
   questions were asked regarding demographics, relationship with the
   territory, current management system, relationship with fire and media,
   risk perception, and others.Results were compared according to the
   critical area or the type of respondents, and several analyses were
   conducted to identify weaknesses, strengths, and areas for improvement
   to raise awareness and lower the risk.BackgroundWildfire is a complex
   chemical, physical, and sociological phenomenon deeply rooted in the
   historical relationship between humans and fire. Today the wildfire risk
   is one of the human challenges. Effective management requires
   collaboration among multiple stakeholders across different levels. The
   risk perception and vulnerability at the local community level explain
   why and how individuals consider certain policies or mitigation
   behaviors. Thus, wildfire risk fits within the framework of
   socio-ecological systems.This study focused on four fire-prone areas in
   Tuscany, Italy, aiming to explore local wildfire risk perception. Risk
   perception is a social parameter, derived from media habits, memory,
   history, concerns, and beliefs.
   Two different surveys were used to consult two groups: experts (e.g.,
   wildfire technicians, policymakers, business activities, and rural
   associations) and non-experts (e.g., random residents, students, and
   tourists), then compared to investigate possible gaps. Several questions
   were asked regarding demographics, relationship with the territory,
   current management system, relationship with fire and media, risk
   perception, and others.Results were compared according to the critical
   area or the type of respondents, and several analyses were conducted to
   identify weaknesses, strengths, and areas for improvement to raise
   awareness and lower the risk.ResultsFindings revealed differences in
   perception, more between the two groups than across locations,
   highlighting gaps that need to be addressed. A general underestimation
   of risk, with an overall optimism, was found in the non-expert group,
   indicating the need for further qualitative research to understand these
   aspects better. The interviews suggest public action as the main
   component to implement change.ConclusionsThe paradigm shift toward
   prevention represents a core change and challenge. An exchange between
   scientific and local knowledge is desirable to address many gaps. We
   propose awareness raising as a possible starting point and to encourage
   collective actions in line with suggestions from the interviewees.
   Continuous monitoring and evaluation of response patterns can inform
   policy adjustments and resource allocation to enhance resilience and
   response effectiveness in future emergencies. Further research could aim
   to develop tools to promote a culture of fire and risk and deepen the
   analysis of risk perception in the most critical areas.
   AntecedentesLos fuegos de vegetaci \& oacute;n son fen \& oacute;menos
   qu \& iacute;micos, f \& iacute;sicos y sociol \& oacute;gicos complejos
   fuertemente enraizados en la relaci \& oacute;n hist \& oacute;rica
   entre fuegos y humanos. Hoy, el riesgo de estos incendios es uno de los
   desaf \& iacute;os que enfrentamos los humanos. El manejo efectivo de
   estos incendios requiere la colaboraci \& oacute;n de diferentes grupos
   de inter \& eacute;s en varios niveles. La percepci \& oacute;n del
   riesgo y vulnerabilidad a nivel de comunidad local explica el por qu \&
   eacute; y el c \& oacute;mo los individuos consideran algunas pol \&
   iacute;ticas y comportamientos para su mitigaci \& oacute;n. El riesgo
   de incendios de vegetaci \& oacute;n encaja, entonces, dentro del marco
   de los sistemas socio-ecol \& oacute;gicos. Este estudio se enfoc \&
   oacute; en cuatro \& aacute;reas proclives a fuegos en la regi \&
   oacute;n de Toscana, Italia, con el objetivo de determinar la percepci
   \& oacute;n local del riesgo de incendios de vegetaci \& oacute;n. Esta
   ``percepci \& oacute;n del riesgo{''} es un par \& aacute;metro social,
   derivado de los h \& aacute;bitos de los medios, de la memoria, de la
   historia, las preocupaciones, y las creencias. Dos relevamientos
   diferentes fueron usados para consultar a dos grupos: expertos (i. e. T
   \& eacute;cnicos en manejo del fuego, gestores pol \& iacute;ticos,
   personas con actividades econ \& oacute;micas, y asociaciones rurales),
   y no-expertos (i. e. residentes en general, estudiantes, y turistas), y
   luego los comparamos para detectar posibles diferencias. Diferentes
   preguntas fueron realizadas relacionadas con la demograf \& iacute;a, la
   relaci \& oacute;n con el territorio, el manejo actual de los recursos,
   la relaci \& oacute;n entre los incendios y los medios, la percepci \&
   oacute;n del riesgo, y otras. Los resultados fueron comparados de
   acuerdo con el \& aacute;rea cr \& iacute;tica o el tipo de
   respondientes, y diversos an \& aacute;lisis fueron conducidos para
   identificar debilidades, fortalezas, y \& aacute;reas de mejoramiento
   para aumentar la concientizaci \& oacute;n y bajar el
